          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My father had a heart attack at 55. What does that mean for my own risk, and how does guidance use family history when deciding about a statin?",
          "answer": "If you notice chest pain or pressure, shortness of breath, pain spreading to the arm, jaw or back, or sudden sweating or nausea, call emergency services right away. Don't wait to see whether it passes.\n\nI'm sorry about your father. The sources I could search don't give an exact age cutoff for \"early onset\" in a first-degree relative, so I can't tell you whether his age of 55 counts. Your clinician can confirm this, so mention it when you talk to them.\n\nThe lipid guidance (\"Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation\") bases statin decisions mainly on an estimated 10-year cardiovascular risk. That estimate uses age, sex, race, total and HDL cholesterol, systolic blood pressure, blood pressure treatment, diabetes and smoking status. Family history of premature cardiovascular disease is a \"risk-enhancing feature.\" According to this guidance, it can shift the decision only for people in the borderline (5% to below 7.5%) or intermediate (7.5% to below 20%) risk bands, and it does not replace the risk estimate. For borderline risk, the guidance favors a statin where risk-enhancing features are present. The \"Coronary artery disease\" reference lists family history as one contributor alongside smoking, blood pressure, cholesterol, diabetes, weight, activity and diet. The guidance I found covers adults aged 40 to 75 without existing heart disease. Whether it applies to you depends on your age and health history, which I don't know.\n\nThe sources don't say how strongly family history predicts risk on its own, so I can't give you an evidence-strength rating. They also don't show any disagreement between authorities about family history and statins.\n\nThings you can do:\n- Ask a clinician to calculate your 10-year cardiovascular risk. Bring your cholesterol and blood pressure numbers if you have them, and tell them your father's age at his heart attack.\n- Ask whether other relatives had heart disease at a young age, since the guidance counts family history broadly.\n- Ask whether a coronary artery calcium score would help if your risk falls in the uncertain middle range. The lipid guidance lists it as a tool for unclear decisions.\n\nI couldn't retrieve the lifestyle guidance (diet, activity and sleep), so I can't cover it. I also can't tell you whether a statin would suit you. That decision belongs to you and your clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
